<h3>What a formal notice needs</h3>
<p>The law (the Digital Millennium Copyright Act) asks for a few specific things. Leave one out and we may have to write back and ask:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Your name, address, phone and email.</li>
  <li>The work you own. A title, a description or a link to where you’ve shown it are all fine.</li>
  <li>The page on this site where the copy appears. The URL is best.</li>
  <li>A statement that you believe, in good faith, that this use isn’t authorized by you, your agent or the law.</li>
  <li>A statement that the information in your notice is accurate, and, under penalty of perjury, that you’re the owner or authorized to act for the owner.</li>
  <li>Your signature. Typing your full name counts.</li>
</ul>
<p>When a notice is complete, we remove the work and email the artist who posted it. The artist gets a copy of your notice, including your name and contact details, so they can respond.</p>

<h3>And here’s where it gets complicated</h3>
<p>Sometimes the notice is wrong. Two artists paint the same lighthouse. A student files a claim on their teacher’s demo piece. Someone confuses “inspired by” with “copied from.”</p>
<p>If your work was taken down and you believe that was a mistake, you can send us a counter-notice with:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Your name, address, phone and signature.</li>
  <li>What was removed and where it appeared.</li>
  <li>A statement, under penalty of perjury, that you believe in good faith it was removed by mistake or misidentification.</li>
  <li>A statement that you consent to the jurisdiction of the federal district court where you live (or, if you’re outside the U.S., any district where we can be found), and that you’ll accept legal papers from the person who filed the original notice.</li>
</ul>
<p>We send your counter-notice, including your name, address, and phone, to the person who complained. Unless they tell us they’ve gone to court, we put the work back up between 10 and 14 business days after we get your counter-notice.</p>
<p>One caution, in both directions: knowingly lying in a notice or a counter-notice can make you liable for damages. Please don’t use this process to settle a grudge.</p>

<h3>Repeat infringers</h3>
<p>Artists are allowed mistakes. Everyone gets one. But if we remove work from the same account three times because of valid copyright notices, we close the account. We may close it sooner if it’s clear someone is posting other people’s work on purpose.</p>
